request.description:
Marks multiple deliveries picked up in one call — for when you collect a batch of parcels from the warehouse at once instead of one at a time. `order_ids` is a required array (min 1) of delivery ids.

Every submitted id is accounted for in the response, never silently dropped: ids you don't own, ids that don't exist, and ids that aren't currently `assigned` all land in `skipped` with a reason instead of failing the whole request.

```json
{
  "succeeded": [
    { "id": "uuid", "order_number": "#1001", "delivery_status": "picked_up", "...": "...(full order object, same shape as Get Delivery Detail)" }
  ],
  "skipped": [
    { "id": "uuid", "reason": "currently delivered" },
    { "id": "uuid", "reason": "not found or not assigned to you" }
  ]
}
```

A 200 is returned even if every id was skipped — check `skipped` rather than relying on the HTTP status to know what happened per order.

Call this after you've loaded your parcels and decided the order you want to deliver them in. `order_ids` must be every id in your chosen order — position in the array is the stop order (1st id = stop 1, etc). All ids must belong to you or the whole request is rejected with a 422.

**This only records the planned sequence — it does not change any order's `delivery_status`.** You still tap Mark Picked Up / Mark Out For Delivery / Mark Delivered on each order yourself as you physically work through your route; checkout just tells you (and the admin dashboard) what order you planned to do them in.

Response:
```json
{
  "route": [ { ...full order objects, sorted by route_sequence... } ],
  "start_order": { ...same shape, this is route[0], your first stop... }
}
```
Every order object in the response (and in Get Delivery Detail / List Deliveries from now on) also includes a `route_sequence` integer field so you can re-render the planned order any time without calling checkout again.
